  "title": "The road east stretched smoothly and was paved for an hour b...",
  "created_at": "2026-03-08T17:02:34.591Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-03-08T17:02:34.591Z",
  "chapter_text": "The road east stretched smoothly and was paved for an hour beyond the city, though neglected for years. Hairline fractures webbed across the asphalt like a map of dried riverbeds, in random spots. The potholes played hide-and-seek as they tried to make time on the journey. The armored Land Cruiser shuddered over each depression, metal bones creaking against the engineered irregularity. \nMiranda rode in the backseat strapped in, every jolt a shock through her spine. Ben gripped the steering wheel with white-knuckled determination, eyes flicking from horizon to shoulder, never settling. Martin sat beside him, voice flat as death, calling out hazards: \"Left pothole, two o'clock—deep enough to damage the suspension.\" Noah was next to Miranda, silent as stone since departure; a silence that meant his mind circled threats he had yet to solve.\nThe sputtering AC fought the heat in vain. Her shirt stuck to her skin at the base of her spine; each bump against the seat made her teeth click in time with the road's steady, predictable rhythm.\n Thirteen hours, Martin had said—one way. Behind that number was no mere distance but a slow attrition.\nMiranda pulled out her phone, the one her mother texted on, thumb hovering over the unread message. She slid it back into her pocket. No comfort here.\nA rut launched them upward. Her shoulder slammed into Noah’s; he didn’t flinch. His gaze stayed locked on a point beyond the windshield—a place where danger waited.\n“You eat?” she forced out.\n“Not hungry.”\n“Doesn’t matter.” She offered him a freeze-dried bar. He didn’t take it.\nHe exhaled huskily. “Is this you taking care of me, Agent Cruz?”\n“DELTA,” she corrected herself, the word too sharp, too rehearsed—her stance declared before she meant to. It landed like a blade.\nHe let that slide. “Right.”\nOutside, the wasted land lay flat and white under the noon sun. Occasional acacia trees cast brittle shadows; clusters of mud-brick huts squatted off the road. A motorcycle roared past carrying three figures and a still-flapping chicken bound between their knees. The driver ignored them; the passenger’s gaze sliced across the tinted glass and rested on Miranda long enough to judge her.\nBen caught her watching. “Memorizing their teeth?” he muttered.\nMartin glanced back. “She’s covering our six.”\n“We’re the only American-profile ride on this hellroad,” Ben growled. “We stick out.” \nMiranda repeated the phrase in her head: American posture. Flat. Waiting for a meeting. Her body remained poised, ready.\n“No reply,” Noah said, and the word sliced the air—sharp, final. Ben shut up.\nThe hours bled past as the sun crept west. Ben crawled over each blind crest. Martin flagged every sinkhole. Miranda mapped time in sun angles, creaks in her lower back, the bitter film that spread in her mouth. Paranoia festered inside the confined metal box, ready to bite at any movement: a bird’s flight, the trembling of leaves.\nShe didn’t scratch the itch between her shoulder blades. She knew that letting it show was an invitation.\nA rope barrier crouched around a blind curve like a lion waiting. Two men in faded fatigues stood still, rifles in loose grips. Ben slowed to a crawl; Martin lowered his window, speaking rapid French. Miranda watched the nearer guard’s weight shift, where his eyes darted, if his fingers twitched.\nHis gaze cut through the windshield and settled on Noah. Then the eyes slid to Miranda. She froze, palms white on her knees. Years of practiced neutrality taught her to be uninteresting—no variables. The guard’s stare flicked across the sweat-darkened collar of her shirt, frowned, then dropped his chin. The rope slackened; they were let through.\nDust rose behind them like a specter. Miranda exhaled.\n“Why’d he study me?” she whispered.\n“Because you don’t belong on this route,” Martin said from the front.\n“That’s not—”\n“He’s right,” Ben agreed. “We look like an anomaly.”\nMiranda swallowed. Their invisibility had been shattered.\nHer phone pinged to life—one bar, then gone. No messages. She stowed it.\nLangford’s anger reverberated in her mind: compressed, simmering, a pressure that built inside sealed containers. She remembered Bill’s knees hitting the floor, the instant of recognition in his eyes before everything broke. Her vision swam. She pressed her forehead against the window, the glass a scalding mirror. Noah reached for her elbow.\n“You okay?” His voice was a diagnostic, checking her vitals.\n“Fine,” she managed. He sighed and returned his attention to the road. She studied his profile, the way he held responsibility like a weight.\nThe sky deepened violet as afternoon began its slow descent. Headlights shattered the dusk when Ben flipped them on and insects slammed into the beams in a furious storm. Martin checked his phone. “No comms. Zero.”\n“In theory,” Noah said, “we knew that. In practice, if we’re hit out here, there is no backup.”\n“Never been more thrilled,” Ben said, voice brittle.\nMiranda’s hand found the sat phone in her bag. Cold plastic, the tiny seal pressed her thumb. She hadn’t checked in—promised herself she’d wait. Noah’s low “Don’t” made her freeze.\n“Don’t what?”\n“Not from the road. Not here. This route’s dirty. You ping that thing and you announce our position.”\n“My obligation—daily report.”\n“Then wait until we control the space.” He looked at her, claiming the word we. “We’re a team.”\nShe felt the moment slip: her authority colliding with his caution. She slid the sat phone back into the pocket.\nThe road crumbled into stones under a moon that pressed heat into the earth. Ben ghosted through every crater. Miranda rocked with each impact, skull rattling against the glass. Sleep hovered at the edges of her mind, but her body burned too fiercely. Behind closed eyes the cell-tower image flickered, the dispersal maps danced like ghosts. Bill’s face haunted the darkness: the split-second before irreversible understanding.\nShe opened her eyes. Noah watched her without judgment—an unspoken acknowledgment of shared damage. \n“Why Batouma?” she asked.\n“A cluster of coordinates surfaces there in signal traffic,” Martin said. “We can’t explain that corridor.”\n“Too vague.”\n“Intentionally,” Ben snapped.\nMiranda’s voice went flat. “I’m DELTA—operational authority. You don’t choose what I know.”\nSilence built. Then Noah: “You’re exhausted.”\n“That’s your solution?”\n“No. You’re exhausted, and you crave variables to grip. I get it.”\n“You ‘get it.’” She tasted bile.\nHe said nothing, and the emptiness of that was worse.\nThe compound materialized at last: a sniff of wood smoke, dull glow of a generator behind stacked blocks and tin roofs. Two armed men met them with guarded stares and led them inside a cinderblock room: one cot, a folding table, a single bulb humming with heat, a fan stirring stale air in circles. Dirt crusted the corners like old blood.\n“No time,” Noah said, dropping his pack. “Five minutes.” Ben and Martin melted back into the night without a word.\nMiranda felt her legs tremble under her. She sank to the cot’s edge and pulled out the sat phone. Fingers kept it from trembling as she dialed Langford.\n“Agent Cruz. Report.”\n“En route. Limited comms. Team intact. Principal secure.”\n“Location.”\nShe hesitated, caught between protocol and patience. Noah’s eyes were unwavering: wait.\n“East of Bamako. On route for a follow-on assessment.”\nSilence ate the line. Then: “You’re deliberately imprecise. No confirmation you’re where you claim.” His tone dropped like a guillotine. “Understand what that does to me?”\n“I understand.”\n“Good. Don’t forget what you are, Cruz. You’re the record. You don’t pick the story.”\nClick. The line died.\nMiranda cradled the phone, raw shame threatening to burn through her. Noah watched, steady. He spoke quietly: “He’s tightening the leash.”\n“You don’t know him,” she snapped.\n“I know leashes.”\nHe was right, but she let it lie.\nThe men’s murmured footsteps outside announced the final moments. Noah dropped a manila envelope on the table, name block-printed across the flap: MIRANDA CRUZ. He stepped back, eyes holding hers. Then he left.\nMiranda peeled it open. A single sheet, clinical in its precision: IF MIRANDA CRUZ IS KILLED IN ACTION. Lines detailing how to spin her death: contacts, statements, burial instructions, media framing. A paragraph on Bill, detached, sterile. At the bottom, two final orders: DELIVER TO LANGFORD IF RECOVERY IS POSSIBLE. IF NOT, BURN.\nHer pulse drummed in her ears. She read it twice, the future carved into precise steps. She looked at Noah, still at the door, sentinel to her unraveling.\n“You wrote this,” she said.\n“Yes.”\n“When?”\n“Before the plane,” he answered quietly. “I read your file.”\n“That means you decided I might die—and drafted my epitaph before we’d met.”\n“Better to prepare than improvise.”\nShe folded the paper, placed it back in the envelope, slid it into her pack. “You don’t get to decide my value.”\nHis jaw twitched. “I’m sorry it hurts.”\nNot sorry she found it. Not sorry he planned to bury her systematically. Just sympathy for the pain.\nShe turned away, pressed her cheek to the rough wall. The cot’s springs dug into her back. The generator’s cough rattled her bones. Outside, Ben’s voice barked through the thin wall: a burner phone chirping to life.\nMiranda’s eyes snapped open. Noah pivoted before Ben spoke—a fraction before the syllables formed.\nBen stepped in, holding the phone like a live wire. Noah’s face flickered as he read the incoming message. Some silent calculation bent his features—tightened eyes, a pulse jumping at his temple.\n“What is it?” Miranda sat up, every sense propped on alert.\nNoah placed the phone on the table, screen up: a single incoming message, the subject line only legible for a heartbeat.\n“Turn around,” he said. Their night fracture was just beginning.",
