  "title": "The city let go of them slowly. Bamako at five-thirty in the...",
  "created_at": "2026-03-10T02:35:07.088Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-03-10T02:35:07.088Z",
  "chapter_text": "The city let go of them slowly.\nBamako at five-thirty in the morning was already awake. Vendors rolling carts into position, motorcycle taxis threading between buses, the smell of charcoal and exhaust layering over everything.\nMiranda watched it from the back seat as Ben pulled onto the main artery heading east, and she told herself that was why she kept looking out the window. To watch. To cover their six. Not because she couldn't look at the road ahead. The Land Rover was new; smelling of plastic wrap and air freshener. She had no idea how it had gotten here so quickly. She assumed billionaires just made things happen, the same way Santa just made presents appear on Christmas morning. \nNoah rode up front. Martin was beside her, door-side, his arm propped on the window ledge, already half-asleep or doing a convincing impression of it. His eyes were closed. His breathing was even. She did not understand how anyone's breathing could be even right now. Sabrina stayed behind to work on getting them more information, she would be checking in via satphone as they got closer. \nShe checked her phone. No signal. She stowed it. Then checked it again four minutes later.\nThe highway east was nothing as she'd expected. Two lanes widened into four as the city sprawled outward, and then the traffic — already aggressive inside Bamako — became something else entirely. Trucks ran loaded past their rated capacity, suspension groaning, listing slightly to one side. Minibuses overtook on blind curves with absolute conviction. A cattle truck had shed part of its load somewhere north of the city; they passed three men in the median attempting to corral a goat while traffic split around them without slowing. \nBen watched the men and the goat as he navigated around them, \"That wasn’t in my bingo card for this trip.\" he said, to no one in particular. He made a slight correction around a pothole-sized patch of missing asphalt. \nFive hours outside of Bamako, Miranda's knee started doing the thing. She pressed her hand down on it. She'd been fine in the hotel. She'd been fine at breakfast — she'd eaten, or moved food around, which was close enough. She'd been fine loading the vehicle in the grey pre-dawn, checking her kit, confirming comms. She'd been trained for fine. Fine was the ground state she operated from. At some point between Bamako and wherever they were now, fine had stopped working. She knew what was out there. \nThat was the problem. Noah had laid it out at breakfast, the imagery, the C-130 sitting south of Batouma like a period at the end of a sentence, the compound that hadn't existed two years ago and now had six buildings and a pond that was the wrong color. She'd looked at the images herself. She'd understood what the algae meant before he finished the sentence. She understood what they were driving toward. \nHer knee resumed.\nSomewhere around the seventh hour, a lorry cut across two lanes without signaling. Ben absorbed the correction without comment, the vehicle barely moving. Martin didn't look up from the map. Noah resettled his arm on the window ledge. Miranda exhaled.\"We’re making good time, right?\" she said. Her voice came out slightly too bright. \"Are we going back today?\"Ben opened one eye. Looked at her sideways.A beat.\"Depends on what we find.\"He closed his eyes again. Then: \"But yeah. Good time.\"Miranda faced forward. Something in her chest unknotted slightly. Not much. Enough.\nThe highway life moved around them in waves. A convoy of NGO vehicles running south in the opposite lane, a school bus painted orange and purple with a sound system audible through closed windows, a man on a motorcycle carrying an entire wooden headboard strapped vertically to his back, leaning forward into the wind like a sail. The world out here was enormous and indifferent and operating on its own logic that had nothing to do with neurotoxins or immortal men or C-130s parked next to blue-green ponds in the middle of nowhere.She kept waiting for that to make her feel better.\nAround the ninth hour they stopped for gas, and she got one bar. She watched it. Watched it drop. It came back. She looked out the window. Noah had gone inside to pay. Ben was stretching his legs. Martin was asleep now, head against the window. Ben was eating something from a foil packet, watching the road with the patient, unfocused attention of someone who'd done a lot of long drives. She took out her other phone, the one Langford gave her. It felt hot and heavy in her hand. She typed.\nGoing northeast. Don't have a destination yet. Team intact. Will report when I have more. \nShe read it back. Every word true. She sent it. Put the phone back into her pocket. Nobody said anything. Ben kept eating. Noah started pumping gas. He glanced at her and for just a second, she caught his gaze. He gave her a short nod. She looked away.\nThe Rover came to an abrupt halt. Miranda looked around to see what the issue was. She didn’t see anything, and turned back to the front. Noah held a gun to her face. She felt the heat on her face as the flash erupted.\nMiranda started herself awake. Martin looked at her questioningly. She gave a weak smile and looked at her watch. Two hours to go, plus or minus. The air was cool inside the Rover, but Miranda could feel sweat pooling on her back. \nThe afternoon heat pressed in through the glass despite the AC. The landscape had simplified itself — flatter, drier, scattered trees throwing sharp shadows at low angles. They occasionally saw parts of the Niger River, shallow here. HIppos wallowed in the river, while antelope watered themselves. \nThe highway was quieter now. The trucks were still there, but spaced further apart. The minibuses had mostly turned off somewhere south. It felt like the world was narrowing.\n\"Can I ask you something?\" she said to Martin.\"Probably.\"\"How long have you worked with him?\"\nMartin smiled. \"Forty years.\"\n\"How many times—\" She stopped. Started again. \"\nMartin said forty-nine.\"\"I stopped counting at thirty,\" Ben said. \"Makes it easier.\"Miranda looked out the window. \"Does it get easier?\"Ben thought about it with what seemed like genuine consideration. She appreciated that he didn't just say yes.\"The first time is the worst,\" he said finally. \"Because you think it's your fault. You run the whole thing back looking for where you went wrong, what you missed, what you should have done.\" He paused. \"Took me a while to understand it wasn't a failure condition. It's just...\" He shrugged. \"Tuesday.\"\"That's insane.\"\"Yeah.\"\"You're very calm about it.\"\"I've had thirty Tuesdays,\" Ben said. \"You've had one. Give it time.\"Noah said nothing. Watched the road.\nHer phone buzzed.Langford.Northeast of what. Who authorized the route change. Call me.She turned the screen off.Ben glanced down at her hand. Said nothing.She put the phone in her bag.\nThe sun had moved behind them by the time the highway degraded to two lanes and the two lanes began to feel like a suggestion. The trees got taller. The road curved through scattered low forest and then opened again into flat scrubland. Ben pointed out a herd of cattle maybe half a kilometer off the road, walking in a loose line toward something only they could see.\"Fulani,\" Martin said, awake again. \"Moving to water.\"Miranda watched them until they were gone.Something in her had gone quiet in the last hour. Not calm — she wasn't going to call it calm. But the talking-too-fast had burned itself out, and the knee had stilled, and she'd stopped checking the phone every four minutes. What was left was something simpler and harder than fear.She was going to walk through that gate. Whatever was inside. That was just true now.She wasn't sure when it had become true, but it had.\nNoah slowed.Ahead, where the road curved slightly south, a concrete wall rose out of the scrubland. Three meters high. The top edge ran with razor wire that caught the last of the sun and threw it back in flat, hard glints. Behind it — just visible above the roofline — the peak of a prefabricated metal building.He pulled off the road. Stopped. Cut the engine.They sat.The gate was open. Not ajar — open. Pushed back to its stops, as if someone had left in a hurry, or wanted them to think so, or wanted them to think about what it meant that they were thinking about it.The sun was low and golden and the shadows were long and everything was very quiet.\"Well,\" Ben said.Nobody answered that.Miranda looked at the gate.Her hands, she noticed, were still.",
