    {
      "key": "focus_character",
      "label": "Focus Character",
      "value": "Miranda — the chapter is filtered almost entirely through her perspective, interiority, and physical anxiety responses."
    },
    {
      "key": "character_beginning_state",
      "label": "Character Beginning State",
      "value": "Performing composure ('Fine was the ground state she operated from') while quietly unraveling — checking her phone compulsively, watching out the back window, unable to face the road ahead. Trained but not yet tested."
    },
    {
      "key": "character_end_state",
      "label": "Character End State",
      "value": "Arrived at a quiet, pre-action resolve — not calm, but beyond fear. Her hands are still. She has internally committed to walking through the gate regardless of what's inside. The performative 'fine' has burned away into something harder and simpler."
    },
    {
      "key": "emotional_arc",
      "label": "Emotional Arc",
      "value": "Suppressed anxiety → physical symptom escalation (knee, compulsive phone-checking, too-bright voice) → brief relief from Ben's reassurance → betrayal anxiety spike (Langford's message, guilt over the burner phone) → dream/nightmare intrusion → quiet burn-out into resolve. A credible descent-and-hardening arc, well-paced."
    },
    {
      "key": "chapter_goal",
      "label": "Chapter Goal",
      "value": "Transport the team from Bamako to the perimeter of the target compound northeast of the city, and bring Miranda (and the reader) to the threshold of the mission's point of no return."
    },
    {
      "key": "central_problem",
      "label": "Central Problem",
      "value": "Miranda must hold herself together during a long approach drive toward a known chemical/biological threat site, while managing split loyalties (team vs. Langford handler relationship) and the psychological weight of understanding exactly what they're driving toward."
    },
    {
      "key": "solution",
      "label": "Solution (if any)",
      "value": "No resolution — the chapter ends at the gate. Miranda's internal problem (fear) is partially resolved through exhaustion and the passage of time, not through action or insight. The external mission problem remains fully open."
    },
    {
      "key": "new_problem",
      "label": "New Problem Introduced",
      "value": "Two: (1) Langford is monitoring the route and is angry — 'Northeast of what. Who authorized the route change. Call me.' — suggesting Miranda is operating outside sanctioned boundaries or that Langford doesn't fully know the plan, creating a loyalty/exposure risk. (2) The compound gate is wide open, which is either an invitation or a trap — both possibilities are equally dangerous."
    },
    {
      "key": "key_conflict",
      "label": "Key Conflict",
      "value": "Internal: Miranda's trained composure vs. her embodied fear of what she knows is ahead. Secondary external conflict: her covert reporting to Langford vs. her operational loyalty to Noah's team — flagged by Noah's loaded glance at the gas station and her decision to ignore Langford's follow-up message."
    },
    {
      "key": "stakes",
      "label": "Stakes",
      "value": "Immediate physical survival (the compound likely contains neurotoxins; the algae color indicates a biological/chemical pond). Broader: whatever the C-130 and compound represent — the 'immortal men' reference suggests the stakes extend well beyond this mission. Miranda's double-agent position also puts her at personal risk from both sides."
    },
    {
      "key": "setting",
      "label": "Setting / Location",
      "value": "Moving through Mali — from Bamako eastward across approximately nine hours of highway, ending at a walled compound in scrubland, possibly near the Niger River bend. Landscape transitions from chaotic urban sprawl to flat, dry savanna to low forest. Vivid, specific, and grounded — the hippos, Fulani cattle, road hazards, and NGO convoys all feel researched rather than generic."
    },
    {
      "key": "tone",
      "label": "Tone / Atmosphere",
      "value": "Controlled dread with moments of wry human texture. The chapter sustains tension through accumulation rather than incident — long drives, small gestures, the world operating indifferently around a covert operation. Ben's 'Tuesday' speech is the emotional center. The final image (open gate, long shadows, still hands) achieves genuine menace through restraint. FLAG: 'immortal men' is dropped as established lore but unexplained to a first-time reader of this chapter — may read as a tonal intrusion of genre-melodrama if context isn't established earlier in the book."
    },
    {
